1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,151
This is Aetheris A I, a browser simulation of an Alexa plus observatory assistant.

2
00:00:06,151 --> 00:00:08,626
It uses synthetic telescope data.

3
00:00:08,626 --> 00:00:11,626
This demonstration uses local rehearsal.

4
00:00:11,626 --> 00:00:16,577
There is no live Alexa connection and no Amazon Web Services call.

5
00:00:17,577 --> 00:00:21,952
The observatory view shows twenty reproducible observations.

6
00:00:21,952 --> 00:00:27,931
Row seven, sector G T C zero zero seven, contains our deliberately injected event.

7
00:00:27,931 --> 00:00:37,264
Its luminosity reaches nine thousand eight hundred arbitrary units, with a thermal variation of twelve hundred and fifty kelvin.

8
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:40,982
This is a test case, not an astronomical discovery.

9
00:00:41,982 --> 00:00:45,654
The observer asks the assistant to scan the observatory.

10
00:00:45,654 --> 00:00:50,571
The working tool processes the same data as the table and creates a report.

11
00:00:50,571 --> 00:00:53,784
The response clearly identifies its local origin.

12
00:00:53,784 --> 00:01:02,373
In the real Bedrock mode, this scan operation would instead send the required prompt and data through the implemented A W S client.

13
00:01:03,373 --> 00:01:06,776
Next, explain the anomaly computes the evidence.

14
00:01:06,776 --> 00:01:13,795
It compares the peak with the median of the other observations, giving a ratio of about ninety six.

15
00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:17,269
The thermal measurement is reported alongside it.

16
00:01:17,269 --> 00:01:23,437
This tool runs locally, so the explanation does not silently create another model call.

17
00:01:23,437 --> 00:01:27,833
A possible supernova is still only a demonstration hypothesis.

18
00:01:28,833 --> 00:01:32,661
Prepare follow up creates a downloadable J S O N draft.

19
00:01:32,661 --> 00:01:42,752
It links the sector, measurements and original scan report, and proposes checks for calibration, additional observations and debris trajectories.

20
00:01:42,752 --> 00:01:45,119
Its state is pending human review.

21
00:01:45,119 --> 00:01:48,668
Nothing is sent and no telescope time is scheduled.

22
00:01:48,668 --> 00:01:53,053
The draft can be inspected before any future authorized action.

23
00:01:54,053 --> 00:01:59,757
The execution trace makes each tool visible, including its arguments and results.

24
00:01:59,757 --> 00:02:05,109
The application remains in one Python file, with twenty six automated tests.

25
00:02:05,109 --> 00:02:12,152
The Bedrock request format is tested with simulated clients, but no real cloud inference is claimed.

26
00:02:12,152 --> 00:02:17,363
Our next step is an authorized cloud test and evaluation with astronomers.
